WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.294
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.294 --> 00:00:10.170
Two summers ago, I was sitting in my
living room glued to the TV screen.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:15.155
The most important race of
the Olympic Games, Rio 2016,

00:00:15.155 --> 00:00:20.890
was about to begin,
the men's 100 meter butterfly swim final.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:27.735
Now, let me set something straight,
I am not a sports fan.

00:00:27.735 --> 00:00:28.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.340
&gt;&gt; I barely even make it to Arillaga Gym

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.500
and I live there just across the street.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.310
&gt;&gt; But this race was different.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.870
The usual big names were swimming.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:43.060
Michael Phelps from the US,
Chad Le Clos from South Africa.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.850
They were the favorites touted to win.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:47.280
It was going to be a very tight race.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:55.450
But these weren't the guys I was
watching the race for, I was rooting for

00:00:55.450 --> 00:01:02.317
a young boy called Joseph Schooling
from a little country called Singapore.

00:01:02.317 --> 00:01:07.440
Now, I want you to watch lane 4 with me.
&gt;&gt; Clear almost.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.100
Can he hold on?
&gt;&gt; Is he going to make it,

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.869
is he going to make it?
&gt;&gt; History in the pool, but

00:01:11.869 --> 00:01:15.093
we are history that comes Singapore.
&gt;&gt; [NOISE] Did you

00:01:15.093 --> 00:01:15.596
see that?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:15.596 --> 00:01:16.567
&gt;&gt; And my necklace is still in place.

00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:17.441
&gt;&gt; An Olympic gold medal, and

00:01:17.441 --> 00:01:20.265
an Olympic record time.
&gt;&gt; Did you guys see that?

00:01:20.265 --> 00:01:22.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.705
&gt;&gt; My jump but also his win.

00:01:23.705 --> 00:01:24.700
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.680
&gt;&gt; Our first Olympic gold as a country.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.560
50 years of independence,
and our first gold.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.960
And I still remember the pride I
felt when they played our anthem and

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.380
raised our flag.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:43.410
The tears that came to my eyes, and I was
just sitting there watching my TV screen.

00:01:45.132 --> 00:01:49.490
That was probably one of the proudest
moments in our country's young history.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.910
But the odds were stacked against us, so

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:58.050
few people thought we will make it to
the final, much less make it to the top.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.998
But our boy, Joseph, he had a dream.

00:02:02.998 --> 00:02:09.160
David against Goliath.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.700
The little country that beat the odds.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:18.160
That day,
Joseph fulfilled his own dream, but

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.790
he also fulfilled the dreams of an entire
nation who pinned its hopes on him.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:29.457
I'm not a competitive athlete,
I'm a public servant, but

00:02:29.457 --> 00:02:33.580
in so many ways,
it's really not that different.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:39.460
I think about how competitive my country
is going to be in the global race for

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:43.590
talent, resources,
investments, development.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:48.281
I think about what nation my country wants
to be and how we're going to get there.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:51.960
How do you build a nation?

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.759
A nation is built on
stories like Joseph's.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:03.001
Stories of dreams, your dreams,
mine, our families,

00:03:03.001 --> 00:03:07.432
our communities, but
also the blood, and the sweat,

00:03:07.432 --> 00:03:12.360
and the tears that we put in
to get us to where we are.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.956
Even Martin Luther King had a dream.

00:03:13.956 --> 00:03:19.280
A nation is built on all those dreams, and

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.790
the discipline, and
the sacrifice that got us here.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.740
Just 50 years ago,
Singapore was a third-world country,

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:34.280
newly independent from the British,
no natural resources.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.600
It was during this time my
grandparents raised a family,

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:39.170
when the country had almost nothing.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.930
There were no industries, no jobs.

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:48.650
It was about survival then,
not about dreams.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:52.489
My mum growing up remembers
one packet of noodles being

00:03:52.489 --> 00:03:54.750
shared amongst five children.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.220
One pair of shoes passed
down from child to child.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:04.550
By the time it got to my youngest aunt,
it was tattered and torn.

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:11.060
My grandfather could only find employment
as a clerk at the police force, and even

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:18.220
then, his meager salary of $200 a month
wasn't enough to raise a family of six,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:22.910
and so he had to double up
as a watchman at night.

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:26.200
Life was tough but that was life then.

00:04:28.832 --> 00:04:31.850
Everyone thought that this
new country would fail.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.140
You couldn't even find us on the map.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:36.100
&gt;&gt; We're that small.

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:40.769
But that didn't deter us.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:44.770
Our leaders were determined
that this little country,

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:48.411
we would not just survive,
we would thrive.

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:55.270
Now, the US had 300 years of development
to get to where it was at that time.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:58.520
We didn't have the luxury of time and so

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:02.480
we embarked on industrialization and
globalization at the same time.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:07.125
Because they knew then that in order for
this country to make it,

00:05:07.125 --> 00:05:11.260
they had to create employment,

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:16.260
create economy, but most of all create
an environment where people could hope,

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.320
could aspire and could dream.

00:05:20.626 --> 00:05:24.631
And so we went out to look for companies.

00:05:24.631 --> 00:05:29.723
We went out to look for investments, and
we realized that the only way anyone was

00:05:29.723 --> 00:05:35.050
going to invest in us was if we offered
them something that other people couldn't.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:40.860
Stability, transparency, and
a pro basis environment.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:46.090
It meant everybody had to fall in line,
including the unions.

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:49.820
And so they came in droves.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:53.156
Some of our earliest investors,
American companies, Hewlett-Packard,

00:05:53.156 --> 00:05:55.530
Texas Instruments,
they're are still there today.

00:05:57.437 --> 00:06:01.858
And they came because of the political
stability, because of the determination of

00:06:01.858 --> 00:06:05.220
the people, and because of
the commitment of the government.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:10.300
So they built factories,
hired thousands of people.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:14.669
People including my family members,
their neighbors and their friends.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:20.420
By their very presence,
these companies required local services.

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:25.040
And so that gave my parents the chance
to be small business owners,

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:31.030
start their own company, be their own
boss, because that was their dream.

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:35.480
And, as Singapore came
from third world to first,

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:40.760
my family also moved up socially and
economically.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:47.530
And by the time they got to me, I could
be anything I wanted to be, I could be

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:52.760
a doctor, an entrepreneur, Miss Universe.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:54.470
&gt;&gt; That was a privilege I grew up with.

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:59.900
I speak three languages,
I've lived on three continents,

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:04.605
I love Shakespeare's soliloquies as
much as I love Elvis's Blue Suede Shoes.

00:07:04.605 --> 00:07:05.860
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:07.400
&gt;&gt; And I'm just as comfortable having

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:10.390
burritos for lunch at Arbuckle
as I am having Chinese food.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:19.890
I am who I am because of my family,
and also because of my country.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:27.910
A country which today has a per capita
GDP comparable to that of the US,

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:31.180
and we're just 5.5 million people.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:34.325
We also have one of the most
spectacular skylines.

00:07:34.325 --> 00:07:34.896
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:34.896 --> 00:07:35.812
&gt;&gt; Not biased.

00:07:35.812 --> 00:07:39.668
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:42.343 --> 00:07:44.465
&gt;&gt; I didn't end up being a doctor or

00:07:44.465 --> 00:07:48.275
an entrepreneur, and
I guess not Miss Universe either.

00:07:48.275 --> 00:07:50.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.342
&gt;&gt; I found my calling in the public

00:07:52.342 --> 00:07:56.003
service, where I get to make
people's dreams come true,

00:07:56.003 --> 00:07:59.815
whether they aspire to be that
entrepreneur, that doctor,

00:07:59.815 --> 00:08:03.425
start their own business,
be the next Mark Zuckerberg.

00:08:03.425 --> 00:08:07.587
Spend the rest of their lives
coding in front of a computer, or

00:08:07.587 --> 00:08:10.963
networking with the world's
business leaders.

00:08:10.963 --> 00:08:14.185
My job is to make those dreams come true.

00:08:14.185 --> 00:08:20.600
I get to build industries, I get to bring
world's best companies to Singapore.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:23.786
Why?
So that I can create the economy,

00:08:23.786 --> 00:08:27.594
the employment, and the environment so

00:08:27.594 --> 00:08:31.740
that these people's dreams can come true.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.020
So we've won the gold.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:38.770
Where do we go from here?

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:45.690
What got us here 50 years ago is not
going to get us to the next 50 years.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:50.705
Economic empowerment brought
us social mobility but

00:08:50.705 --> 00:08:55.270
it also brought a whole host of
challenges, like social inequality.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:09:01.400
And we have to address those challenges
as we build the next 50 years,

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:06.860
the next 100 years, as we continue to
build the country for people to dream.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:13.449
We started off as a nation
that beat the odds.

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:22.630
We did it with discipline,
hard work, sacrifice.

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:27.607
When Joseph Schooling won that
gold medal and he brought it home,

00:09:27.607 --> 00:09:33.230
he embodied not just our dreams, but
all the values that make us a nation.

00:09:34.853 --> 00:09:39.412
50 years ago, we would never have imagine

00:09:39.412 --> 00:09:43.859
the day we would win
an Olympic gold medal.

00:09:43.859 --> 00:09:50.491
And we did, with our dreams and
with our values.

00:09:51.994 --> 00:09:57.918
And, if Singapore can beat the odds,
this little country,

00:09:57.918 --> 00:10:03.390
then perhaps other cities and
other countries can too.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:09.486
After all a nation is built on dreams.

00:10:09.486 --> 00:10:13.166
Thank you.

00:10:13.166 --> 00:10:18.810
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:22.811
[MUSIC]

